WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.992 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.992 --> 00:00:03.984 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:21.507 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:21.507 --> 00:00:23.090 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: So
let's get started.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.610 align:middle line:84%
Last time, at the end
of the last lecture,

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.150 align:middle line:90%
we introduced outer measure.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.670 align:middle line:84%
So we first discussed
what we wanted

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:33.107 align:middle line:84%
to measure, the
properties of kind

00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:37.240 align:middle line:84%
of a notion measure of
subsets of to satisfy.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.780 align:middle line:84%
We wanted it to, first off,
be defined for all subsets.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.120 align:middle line:84%
We then wanted the
measure of an interval

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.170 align:middle line:84%
to be the length
of the interval.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:51.520 align:middle line:84%
We also wanted the measure of
a union of disjoint subsets

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.490 align:middle line:84%
to be the sum of the
measures, and said

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.440 align:middle line:84%
we wanted it to be
translation invariant,

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.890 align:middle line:84%
meaning if I take a set and
I shift it by a fixed amount,

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.290 align:middle line:84%
then the measure
of the shifted set

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:09.320 align:middle line:84%
is the same as the measure
of the original set.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.380 align:middle line:84%
But as I said last
time, that's impossible.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.150 align:middle line:90%
You can't.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.040 align:middle line:84%
There does not
exist such a thing

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.270 align:middle line:84%
defined on every
subset of real numbers.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:25.000 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to do is define--

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.040 align:middle line:84%
or what we're doing right now
is defining outer measure, which

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.790 align:middle line:84%
will satisfy almost
all of the properties

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:39.580 align:middle line:84%
we wanted, and is defined
for every subset of R.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.640 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll restrict
this outer measure

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.550 align:middle line:84%
to a certain class
of subsets of R

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.730 align:middle line:84%
that are well behaved
with respect to measure.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:54.580 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll get a
function, now defined

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:58.360 align:middle line:84%
on a collection of
subsets of R, what

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.160 align:middle line:90%
we'll call measurable sets.

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.730 align:middle line:84%
And this set function, which we
call measure, Lebesgue measure,

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.060 align:middle line:84%
will satisfy the three
properties, the main three

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.750 align:middle line:84%
properties we wanted-- that
the measure of an interval

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.500 align:middle line:84%
is the length of the
interval, the measure

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.610 align:middle line:84%
of a countable
disjoint union of sets

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.680 align:middle line:84%
is the sum of the measures,
and it's translation invariant.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:26.100 align:middle line:84%
Now at the end of last time--
so we defined outer measure

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.900 align:middle line:84%
and we also proved
this theorem that if we

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.510 align:middle line:84%
have a countable
collection of subsets of R,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.210 align:middle line:84%
then the measure of
the union is less than

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:34.770 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the sum
of the measures.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, what we would like
in the end, like I said,

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.650 align:middle line:84%
is to be able to have
equality whenever

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.600 align:middle line:90%
the subsets are disjoint.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.120 align:middle line:84%
Now, this doesn't hold for
outer measure, but as we'll see,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.560 align:middle line:84%
once we restrict outer measure
to a certain class of subsets,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.390 align:middle line:84%
we will get the
property that we want.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:58.970 align:middle line:84%
Now outer measure
almost satisfies

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.420 align:middle line:84%
one of those properties we
want to have of a measure.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:03.890 align:middle line:84%
What we're going
to verify now is

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.880 align:middle line:84%
that outer measure does satisfy
one of the properties we wanted

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.490 align:middle line:84%
of a measure, namely that
the measure of a interval

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:11.610 align:middle line:90%
ought to be its length.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:15.820 align:middle line:90%
So that's the following--

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:25.840 align:middle line:84%
if I is an interval, then the
measure, the outer measure

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.460 align:middle line:84%
of I, is equal to the length of
I. So here, the length of I--

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:33.760 align:middle line:84%
remember, if it's an
interval of the form a, b,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.600 align:middle line:84%
with the endpoints included or
not, the length of the interval

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:38.470 align:middle line:90%
is b minus a.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:44.094 align:middle line:84%
If it's an infinite interval,
then the length is infinite.

00:03:44.094 --> 00:03:50.630 align:middle line:90%


00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:59.750 align:middle line:84%
So the most involved part, or at
least the crux of proving this,

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.780 align:middle line:84%
is doing the simplest
kind of interval,

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.130 align:middle line:84%
which is a closed and
bounded interval, a, b.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:10.430 align:middle line:90%
So let's do that case first.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:15.125 align:middle line:90%
Suppose I is equal to a, b.

00:04:15.125 --> 00:04:24.330 align:middle line:90%


00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.960 align:middle line:84%
And also, something that I
didn't write down here is we

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.880 align:middle line:84%
have, immediately from the
definition of outer measure--

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.860 align:middle line:84%
so let me just pause
that real quick--

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.350 align:middle line:84%
that if A is a subset of B,
then the outer measure of A

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.440 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the outer measure of B,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:49.270 align:middle line:84%
simply because if I
take any open collection

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.130 align:middle line:84%
of open intervals
covering B, then

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:54.010 align:middle line:84%
that will be a collection
of open intervals covering

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:58.120 align:middle line:84%
A. And since infimums
of bigger sets--

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:02.875 align:middle line:90%


00:05:02.875 --> 00:05:04.650 align:middle line:84%
let's see, which
direction is that--

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.450 align:middle line:90%


00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:11.350 align:middle line:84%
since infimums of bigger
sets have to decrease,

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.390 align:middle line:84%
that gives me the
inequality that I want.

00:05:13.390 --> 00:05:18.860 align:middle line:90%


00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.460 align:middle line:84%
So going back to the
proof of this theorem,

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.780 align:middle line:90%
let's suppose I equals a, b.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.350 align:middle line:84%
What we're going to show
is that the outer measure

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:29.300 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the length of I,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.300 align:middle line:84%
and then the converse--
that the length of I

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.610 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to
the outer measure of I.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.760 align:middle line:84%
So the simpler one is showing
the outer measure of I

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.800 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the length of I.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:45.170 align:middle line:84%
So then I is contained
in the open interval,

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:52.420 align:middle line:84%
the single open interval,
I minus epsilon,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.900 align:middle line:84%
b plus epsilon for
all epsilon positive.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.750 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, since
the outer measure of I

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:03.490 align:middle line:84%
is the infimum of the sum of
lengths of intervals covering

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:07.570 align:middle line:84%
I, this implies that
the outer measure of I

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:19.270 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
length of this interval, which

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:22.210 align:middle line:84%
is equal to b minus
a plus 2 epsilon

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:25.120 align:middle line:90%
for all epsilon positive.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.050 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I have this
is less than or equal to b

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.870 align:middle line:84%
minus a plus to epsilon
for all epsilon positive,

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.680 align:middle line:84%
I can then send epsilon
to 0 to conclude

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.890 align:middle line:84%
that the outer measure of
the interval is less than

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:44.420 align:middle line:84%
or equal to b minus a, the
length of the interval.

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:45.590 align:middle line:90%
So that's the simple part.

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.420 align:middle line:90%


00:06:48.420 --> 00:07:01.640 align:middle line:84%
So now, we're going to show
that b minus a is less than

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:07.785 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the outer
measure of the interval.

00:07:07.785 --> 00:07:10.890 align:middle line:90%


00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.320 align:middle line:84%
So also, what this shows
is that the outer measure

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.700 align:middle line:90%
of this interval is finite.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.700 align:middle line:90%
It's a finite number.

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:21.750 align:middle line:84%
So to show this,
what we have to show

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:26.820 align:middle line:84%
is that if I take any collection
of open intervals covering I,

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.220 align:middle line:84%
then the sum of the
lengths of those intervals

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.155 align:middle line:90%
is bounded below by b minus a.

00:07:31.155 --> 00:07:38.910 align:middle line:90%


00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:41.250 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't write n
equals 1 to infinity,

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.050 align:middle line:84%
it should be understood
that n is always

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.100 align:middle line:90%
going from 1 to infinity.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:57.380 align:middle line:84%
Let I n be a collection
of open intervals

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:07.890 align:middle line:84%
such that this interval I, a,
b, is contained in the union.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.710 align:middle line:84%
We now want to show that b
minus a is less than or equal

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.660 align:middle line:84%
to the sum of the
measures of the I n's.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, the closed and bounded
interval, a, b-- this

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.240 align:middle line:90%
is a compact set.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.620 align:middle line:84%
So you look back
to 100B, which is

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.200 align:middle line:84%
covered by a collection
of open intervals--

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.370 align:middle line:90%
in other words, open sets.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.640 align:middle line:90%


00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:33.890 align:middle line:90%
The definition of compactness--

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:43.130 align:middle line:84%
so since this closed and
bounded interval is compact,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:53.760 align:middle line:84%
this is due to a special
theorem by Heine and Borel--

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.170 align:middle line:84%
we can find a finite
covering of this interval

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.630 align:middle line:84%
by finitely many
of these intervals.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:06.960 align:middle line:84%
So I can choose finitely many of
these intervals to cover a, b.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.920 align:middle line:84%
There exists a
finite collection,

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:17.310 align:middle line:84%
which I will now denote, let's
say, J k, k equals 1 to n,

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:25.640 align:middle line:84%
so contained inside this
collection of open intervals--

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.430 align:middle line:84%
so these are just finitely
many open intervals from this

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:29.210 align:middle line:90%
collection--

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:32.290 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:42.088 align:middle line:84%
such that a, b is contained
in k equals 1 to n J k.

00:09:42.088 --> 00:09:52.490 align:middle line:90%


00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.930 align:middle line:84%
So now, I have these
open intervals.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:59.510 align:middle line:84%
And now what I'm going to
argue is that I can do this--

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:03.680 align:middle line:90%
is that here's the plan.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:05.840 align:middle line:90%
Here's a.

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:07.460 align:middle line:90%
There's b.

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:08.930 align:middle line:84%
What I'm going to
argue now to you

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:17.700 align:middle line:84%
is that rearranging how I'm
indexing these open intervals,

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:24.480 align:middle line:84%
that I can cover a, b
in the following way--

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:34.180 align:middle line:90%
so this is the first.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.950 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to argue that
we can cover this interval

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:42.060 align:middle line:84%
like this, so that I can choose
the first interval to cover

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.880 align:middle line:84%
some of a, b, and then maybe
it covers all, in which case

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:47.790 align:middle line:90%
I would stop my construction.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:54.180 align:middle line:84%
If it doesn't cover all of a,
b, then I can cover it with J2.

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:58.530 align:middle line:90%
So now, I have a2, b2.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:01.140 align:middle line:84%
And if that doesn't
cover all of a, b,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:06.270 align:middle line:84%
I will still have to choose
some more intervals to cover it.

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:10.380 align:middle line:90%
And eventually, I'll get to--

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.430 align:middle line:84%
at least in this picture, since
I can't draw n intervals--

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.640 align:middle line:84%
I will be able to cover
a, b in such a way

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:32.610 align:middle line:84%
that these are kind
of linked together,

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:37.100 align:middle line:84%
so that these
intervals are linked.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:39.980 align:middle line:90%
And then I cover all of a, b.

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:41.240 align:middle line:90%
Now, why is that great?

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:44.870 align:middle line:84%
Well, then, because the sum of
the lengths of these intervals

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.510 align:middle line:84%
is going to be bigger
than or equal to--

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.330 align:middle line:84%
the sum of the lengths of these
intervals is going to be what?

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:56.000 align:middle line:84%
b3 minus a1, which is bigger
than or equal to b minus a.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.530 align:middle line:84%
And that would give me the
lower bound that I desire.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.370 align:middle line:84%
So now, let me argue
that we can do this--

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.960 align:middle line:90%
that this picture is correct.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:10.090 align:middle line:90%


00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.250 align:middle line:90%
Since a is in this--

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:15.870 align:middle line:90%


00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:18.930 align:middle line:84%
it's in a, b, so it has
to be in this finite union

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.730 align:middle line:90%
of open intervals--

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:32.810 align:middle line:84%
there exists k1 such
that a is in J of k1.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:36.270 align:middle line:84%
So one of these intervals,
a has to be in it.

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:54.210 align:middle line:84%
And by rearranging
the intervals,

00:12:54.210 --> 00:13:05.710 align:middle line:84%
I can assume that k1 equals 1,
i.e., a is in J1, which is a1,

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:06.210 align:middle line:90%
b1.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:14.420 align:middle line:90%


00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:18.860 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's possible that
this whole interval

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:24.650 align:middle line:84%
covers a, b, in which case I
would stop the construction.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:25.775 align:middle line:90%
Otherwise, I continue.

00:13:25.775 --> 00:13:28.580 align:middle line:90%


00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:34.870 align:middle line:84%
If b1 is less than
or equal to b,

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:42.190 align:middle line:84%
then b1 is certainly in
this interval, which implies

00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:45.935 align:middle line:90%
there exists a J k sub 2--

00:13:45.935 --> 00:13:49.410 align:middle line:90%


00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:50.760 align:middle line:90%
let me write it this way--

00:13:50.760 --> 00:14:00.940 align:middle line:84%
there exists k sub 2 such
that b1 is in j sub k sub 2.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:09.740 align:middle line:84%
And again, by
rearranging, we can

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:18.650 align:middle line:90%
assume that k sub 2 equals 2.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.690 align:middle line:84%
So by re-arranging the
remaining intervals,

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.730 align:middle line:90%
I can assume k2 is 2.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:25.430 align:middle line:84%
So if in the case, like
I've drawn in the picture,

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:30.380 align:middle line:84%
b1 is not bigger than b, then
I can find another interval J2

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:33.050 align:middle line:90%
that contains b1.

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:45.750 align:middle line:90%


00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:56.780 align:middle line:90%
So b1 is in J2, which is a2, b2.

00:14:56.780 --> 00:15:05.250 align:middle line:84%
And I will just continue this
until the endpoint of one

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.830 align:middle line:90%
of these intervals passes b.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:11.070 align:middle line:84%
The first instance when one
of these intervals passes b, I

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:12.660 align:middle line:90%
stop this process.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:16.320 align:middle line:84%
And it has to stop because all
of these intervals do cover b,

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:17.910 align:middle line:84%
so it has to occur
at some point.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.870 align:middle line:84%
And there's only
finitely many intervals.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:23.770 align:middle line:90%


00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:29.080 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to write if b2
is less than or equal to b,

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:31.730 align:middle line:84%
but I'm going to put
dot, dot, dot there.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.180 align:middle line:84%
So what have we done
by this argument?

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:38.800 align:middle line:90%


00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:51.370 align:middle line:90%
Thus, we conclude that--

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:53.470 align:middle line:84%
maybe that's a little
bit bad notation

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.510 align:middle line:90%
because I have n there.

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:58.090 align:middle line:84%
So let's make this a
capital N. Sorry about that.

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:04.760 align:middle line:90%


00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:08.930 align:middle line:84%
Make that a capital N, since
that little n appears also

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.325 align:middle line:90%
to index the I sub n's.

00:16:10.325 --> 00:16:13.490 align:middle line:90%


00:16:13.490 --> 00:16:21.490 align:middle line:84%
So thus, we conclude that there
exists a k with 1 less than

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:27.340 align:middle line:84%
or equal to k less
than or equal to n such

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:33.160 align:middle line:90%
that three things hold.

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:38.260 align:middle line:84%
For all k equals
1 to k minus 1, we

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:51.820 align:middle line:84%
have that b k is less
than or equal to b and a k

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:58.270 align:middle line:84%
plus 1 is less than b k
is less than b k plus 1.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:03.310 align:middle line:84%
So think of this as a condition
where we have not yet covered

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:04.854 align:middle line:90%
the entire interval a, b.

00:17:04.854 --> 00:17:07.510 align:middle line:90%


00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:12.155 align:middle line:84%
For k equals 1,
this was basically

00:17:12.155 --> 00:17:13.405 align:middle line:90%
the argument I just gave here.

00:17:13.405 --> 00:17:16.510 align:middle line:90%


00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:20.240 align:middle line:84%
And two, that this has
to stop at some point,

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:32.260 align:middle line:84%
so that b is less than b
capital K. So what I drew here--

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:39.830 align:middle line:84%
so the picture is for
the K is K equals 3.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:53.000 align:middle line:90%


00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.873 align:middle line:84%
So we have this K, and I drew
the picture over there for b3.

00:17:55.873 --> 00:17:57.290 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to
show that the sum

00:17:57.290 --> 00:18:01.100 align:middle line:84%
of the links of the
intervals-- and this

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:04.713 align:middle line:84%
is just a finite
collection from the bigger

00:18:04.713 --> 00:18:06.380 align:middle line:84%
collection of intervals--
is now bounded

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:08.660 align:middle line:84%
by b minus a, which is kind
of clear from the picture

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:09.160 align:middle line:90%
that I drew.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:11.120 align:middle line:84%
But we still have
to write stuff down.

00:18:11.120 --> 00:18:12.830 align:middle line:90%
This is not Topology.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:19.760 align:middle line:84%
Then sum of length
of I sub n's--

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:22.160 align:middle line:84%
this is certainly less
than or equal to the sum

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:34.010 align:middle line:84%
over this finite subcollection
of intervals, lengths of J k.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:39.380 align:middle line:84%
And this is less than
or equal to sum k, K--

00:18:39.380 --> 00:18:42.290 align:middle line:84%
now here, this capital K is
coming from over there-- length

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:44.810 align:middle line:90%
of J k.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:46.040 align:middle line:90%
And now what is this?

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:54.170 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to b
K minus a K plus b K

00:18:54.170 --> 00:19:00.050 align:middle line:84%
minus 1 minus a K minus 1
plus, and then all the way

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:11.140 align:middle line:90%
down to b1 minus a1.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:16.400 align:middle line:90%


00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:23.150 align:middle line:84%
But here's the trick-- remember,
each of the b K minus 1's,

00:19:23.150 --> 00:19:27.470 align:middle line:84%
they lie ahead of a K. So the
index here is shifted just

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:28.140 align:middle line:90%
by a little bit.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:37.010 align:middle line:84%
But this says that the previous
b is in front of the next a.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:45.110 align:middle line:84%
So I can collect terms and write
this as b K plus b K minus 1

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:54.960 align:middle line:84%
minus a K plus b K minus
2 minus a K minus 1.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:58.120 align:middle line:84%
So I just borrowed a b
coming after this term,

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:03.870 align:middle line:84%
so on, and so on, until
I get b1 minus a2.

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:08.710 align:middle line:90%
And then I'm stuck with a a1.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:13.630 align:middle line:84%
And by the second condition over
here, everything in parentheses

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:14.840 align:middle line:90%
is non-negative.

00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:21.710 align:middle line:84%
So this is bigger than
or equal to b K minus a1.

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:22.700 align:middle line:90%
And what do we know?

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:27.130 align:middle line:84%
We know b K is bigger than b,
and we know a1 is less than a

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:29.920 align:middle line:90%
because a is in a1, b1.

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:34.360 align:middle line:84%
So this length is
bigger than b minus a.

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:40.630 align:middle line:90%


00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:44.740 align:middle line:84%
And since we've shown that the
sum of lengths of intervals

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:49.180 align:middle line:84%
covering I is bigger than or
equal to b minus a no matter

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.770 align:middle line:84%
what the collection
of intervals is,

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:52.600 align:middle line:84%
then the infimum has
to be bigger than

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:54.130 align:middle line:90%
or equal to b minus a.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:21:00.520 align:middle line:84%
And we conclude that the outer
measure of this interval a, b,

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.165 align:middle line:84%
is bigger than or
equal to b minus a.

00:21:04.165 --> 00:21:07.900 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I have both
sides of the inequality I want,

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:09.550 align:middle line:90%
and therefore, I have equality.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:24.200 align:middle line:90%


00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, this was for a closed
and bounded interval.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:33.030 align:middle line:84%
But this essentially gives us
the result for any interval.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:58.820 align:middle line:84%
So if I use any finite interval
of the form a, b, a, b,

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:12.440 align:middle line:84%
not including a,
open, and then for all

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:15.360 align:middle line:84%
epsilon positive, at
least sufficiently small,

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:16.230 align:middle line:90%
what do I have?

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:23.240 align:middle line:84%
I have that a plus
epsilon, b minus epsilon

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:32.300 align:middle line:84%
is contained in I, which is
contained in a minus epsilon b

00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:34.110 align:middle line:90%
plus epsilon.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:35.880 align:middle line:90%
I is one of these intervals.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:37.400 align:middle line:84%
So if I just make
it a little fatter

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:42.295 align:middle line:84%
and take that closed interval
containing it, I get this side.

00:22:42.295 --> 00:22:43.670 align:middle line:84%
And then if I just
kind of shrink

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:46.640 align:middle line:84%
the interval a little bit
and take the closed interval,

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:51.200 align:middle line:84%
this guy will be
contained inside of it.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:53.990 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
measure of this interval

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:56.150 align:middle line:84%
has to be less than or
equal to the outer measure

00:22:56.150 --> 00:22:57.650 align:middle line:84%
of this interval,
which is less than

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:00.090 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the outer
measure of this interval.

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:01.640 align:middle line:90%
And I get that--

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:04.590 align:middle line:84%
now, the measure
of this interval--

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:05.465 align:middle line:90%
I'll just write this.

00:23:05.465 --> 00:23:21.980 align:middle line:90%


00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:31.150 align:middle line:84%
And this over here is b
minus a minus 2 epsilon

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:36.200 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
outer measure of the interval,

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:39.610 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or equal
to the outer measure of b

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:41.990 align:middle line:90%
minus a plus 2 epsilon.

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:45.670 align:middle line:90%


00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:48.190 align:middle line:84%
And this holds for
all epsilon positive.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:51.600 align:middle line:90%


00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:53.820 align:middle line:84%
Recall that I was starting
off for all epsilon

00:23:53.820 --> 00:23:56.490 align:middle line:84%
positive, at least sufficiently
small depending on b and a,

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.160 align:middle line:84%
smaller than the
difference of b and a.

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:02.142 align:middle line:90%


00:24:02.142 --> 00:24:07.350 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I send
epsilon to 0, I get b minus a

00:24:07.350 --> 00:24:11.070 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
outer measure of this interval,

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:15.240 align:middle line:84%
this finite interval is
less than or equal to--

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:16.860 align:middle line:90%
what why do I have this here?

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:19.990 align:middle line:90%


00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:23.730 align:middle line:84%
Is less than or
equal to b minus a.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:31.110 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the
outer measure of any one

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:34.140 align:middle line:84%
of these finite intervals is
the length of the interval.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:41.190 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to leave
it to you as an exercise,

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:50.670 align:middle line:84%
which is not difficult. If I
is equal to R negative infinity

00:24:50.670 --> 00:25:02.210 align:middle line:90%
to a, a infinity, or include--

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:10.550 align:middle line:90%


00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:18.300 align:middle line:84%
then the outer measure of
this interval is infinity.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:24.430 align:middle line:90%


00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:27.280 align:middle line:84%
In other words, I cannot
ever cover these intervals

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:31.830 align:middle line:84%
by a countable collection of
intervals whose sum of lengths

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:35.200 align:middle line:90%
is a finite number.

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.280 align:middle line:90%
Again, this is not hard.

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:50.920 align:middle line:90%


00:25:50.920 --> 00:25:55.090 align:middle line:84%
So we get kind of
a nice, little fact

00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:59.530 align:middle line:84%
from the theorem we
proved last time, which

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:03.130 align:middle line:84%
was that if I have a
collection of subsets of R,

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:04.990 align:middle line:84%
then the measure of
the union is less

00:26:04.990 --> 00:26:06.698 align:middle line:84%
than or equal to the
sum of the measures,

00:26:06.698 --> 00:26:10.120 align:middle line:84%
and this theorem, which says
that the measure of an interval

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:14.140 align:middle line:84%
is the length of the interval,
which is the following--

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:25.090 align:middle line:90%


00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:35.860 align:middle line:84%
for every subset of R
and epsilon positive,

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:45.060 align:middle line:84%
there exists an open set O
such that A is contained in O

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:49.590 align:middle line:84%
and the measure of
A, which is less than

00:26:49.590 --> 00:26:51.300 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
measure of O simply

00:26:51.300 --> 00:26:54.450 align:middle line:84%
because A is contained
in O, is less than

00:26:54.450 --> 00:27:01.420 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the outer
measure of A plus epsilon.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:06.760 align:middle line:84%
So somehow, the outer
measure of subsets

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:13.450 align:middle line:84%
can be approximately measured by
the outer measure of open sets.

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:16.170 align:middle line:84%
So another way to say this
is, at least with respect

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:19.950 align:middle line:84%
to outer measure,
every set can be

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:23.250 align:middle line:84%
approximated by an open
set, an outer measure.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:25.650 align:middle line:90%
And what's the proof of this?

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:34.930 align:middle line:84%
So this is clear if the
outer measure is infinite.

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:37.420 align:middle line:84%
Then I just take O to be
the entire real number line.

00:27:37.420 --> 00:27:40.020 align:middle line:90%


00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:45.565 align:middle line:90%
So suppose it is finite.

00:27:45.565 --> 00:27:49.710 align:middle line:90%


00:27:49.710 --> 00:28:05.720 align:middle line:84%
Let I n be a collection
of open intervals such

00:28:05.720 --> 00:28:12.053 align:middle line:90%
that they cover A.

00:28:12.053 --> 00:28:14.230 align:middle line:84%
And remember, the outer
measure is the infimum.

00:28:14.230 --> 00:28:16.840 align:middle line:84%
So I can get arbitrarily
close to that infimum

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:21.580 align:middle line:84%
by summing lengths of certain
collections of open intervals.

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:32.340 align:middle line:84%
And sum of the links is
less than or equal to,

00:28:32.340 --> 00:28:36.305 align:middle line:84%
if you like, the outer
measure of A plus epsilon.

00:28:36.305 --> 00:28:40.745 align:middle line:90%


00:28:40.745 --> 00:28:42.370 align:middle line:84%
So I should have said
at the beginning,

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:44.500 align:middle line:84%
let A be a subset of R
and epsilon be positive,

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:48.712 align:middle line:90%
but you understand.

00:28:48.712 --> 00:28:51.950 align:middle line:90%


00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:54.040 align:middle line:84%
And so what I do is
I just take O to be

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.790 align:middle line:90%
this union of open intervals.

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:06.140 align:middle line:90%


00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:10.290 align:middle line:84%
So O is a union
of open intervals.

00:29:10.290 --> 00:29:12.600 align:middle line:84%
Each of these open
intervals is an open set.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:16.020 align:middle line:84%
And you should know that
the union of open sets,

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:18.210 align:middle line:84%
any collection of open sets,
is, again, an open set.

00:29:18.210 --> 00:29:19.020 align:middle line:90%
So this is open.

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:24.640 align:middle line:90%


00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:31.270 align:middle line:84%
A is contained in O because
A is contained in the union.

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:38.990 align:middle line:84%
And the outer
measure of O, which

00:29:38.990 --> 00:29:46.580 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer measure
of this union of open intervals

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:49.850 align:middle line:84%
is, by the theorem we
proved last time, which

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:52.820 align:middle line:84%
I stated with the
definition, or when

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:55.970 align:middle line:84%
I recall the definition is
less than or equal to the sum

00:29:55.970 --> 00:30:01.700 align:middle line:84%
of the outer measures of
the intervals, which we just

00:30:01.700 --> 00:30:06.140 align:middle line:84%
proved is the length
of these intervals.

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:09.440 align:middle line:84%
And how we chose these
intervals, remember,

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:11.570 align:middle line:84%
is so that we have
this condition.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:13.265 align:middle line:84%
And this is less
than or equal to--

00:30:13.265 --> 00:30:17.830 align:middle line:90%


00:30:17.830 --> 00:30:22.420 align:middle line:84%
so with respect
to outer measure,

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:30.580 align:middle line:84%
every set can be approximated by
the approximation with respect

00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:34.060 align:middle line:84%
to outer measure by an open
set, by a suitable open set.

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:50.750 align:middle line:90%


00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:53.312 align:middle line:90%
So we've defined outer measure.

00:30:53.312 --> 00:30:54.770 align:middle line:84%
We've proven some
properties of it.

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:58.610 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're in a position to at
least define measurable sets.

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:05.500 align:middle line:90%


00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:08.310 align:middle line:84%
And I should say these are
Lebesgue measurable sets.

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:10.910 align:middle line:90%


00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:30.630 align:middle line:84%
So a subset of real numbers
is Lebesgue measurable--

00:31:30.630 --> 00:31:36.330 align:middle line:84%
so this is a new
piece of terminology--

00:31:36.330 --> 00:31:46.690 align:middle line:84%
if for all subsets of R, if I
look at the outer measure of A,

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:50.410 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to
the outer measure

00:31:50.410 --> 00:31:58.700 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect E plus the outer
measure of E A intersect E

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:00.860 align:middle line:90%
complement.

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:05.150 align:middle line:84%
This is the definition of being
a Lebesgue measurable set.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:09.350 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, E
is a well-behaved set

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:16.500 align:middle line:84%
if it cuts A into
reasonable sets.

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:18.030 align:middle line:90%
What's the best way to say that?

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:19.440 align:middle line:90%
I guess that's OK.

00:32:19.440 --> 00:32:22.370 align:middle line:90%


00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:27.200 align:middle line:84%
So a set is measurable
if and only if for all A,

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:29.045 align:middle line:90%
we have this equality here.

00:32:29.045 --> 00:32:32.140 align:middle line:90%


00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.500 align:middle line:90%
So let me make a few remarks.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:42.010 align:middle line:90%


00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:48.380 align:middle line:84%
First off, this is
the left-hand side.

00:32:48.380 --> 00:32:52.490 align:middle line:84%
No matter what A and E
are, the left-hand side

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:55.400 align:middle line:84%
is always less than or
equal to the right-hand side

00:32:55.400 --> 00:33:01.625 align:middle line:84%
by the theorem we have up
there, that the outer measure

00:33:01.625 --> 00:33:03.500 align:middle line:84%
of the union is less
than or equal to the sum

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:04.410 align:middle line:90%
of the measures.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:20.710 align:middle line:84%
So since for all A E, A is
contained in, or in fact,

00:33:20.710 --> 00:33:34.310 align:middle line:84%
equal to A intersect E union
A intersect E complement,

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:37.760 align:middle line:84%
we get that the
outer measure of A

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:41.450 align:middle line:84%
is always less than or
equal to the outer measure

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:47.360 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect E plus the
outer measure of A intersect

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:52.430 align:middle line:84%
the complemented of E. This is
regardless of A and E. I mean,

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:55.820 align:middle line:84%
if E is measurable or
not, this always holds.

00:33:55.820 --> 00:34:04.120 align:middle line:84%
So since this always holds, we
could state being measurable

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:07.330 align:middle line:84%
just by, instead of
equality, satisfying

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:09.159 align:middle line:90%
one of the inequalities.

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:18.340 align:middle line:84%
Thus, E is measurable
if for all subsets of R,

00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:32.100 align:middle line:84%
we have that A is less than
or equal to the outer measure

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:36.810 align:middle line:84%
of A. Because like I
said, we know no matter

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.600 align:middle line:90%
what A and E are, this holds.

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:43.139 align:middle line:84%
So if I want equality, then I
have to have this as less than

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:43.900 align:middle line:90%
or equal to that.

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:47.580 align:middle line:84%
So E is measurable if and
only if this is less than

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:56.550 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
outer measure of A.

00:34:56.550 --> 00:35:01.080 align:middle line:90%
So now we have the silliest--

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:04.670 align:middle line:90%


00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:07.520 align:middle line:84%
well, let's not state
it as an example,

00:35:07.520 --> 00:35:10.500 align:middle line:84%
but let's we can
state it as a theorem.

00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:14.440 align:middle line:90%


00:35:14.440 --> 00:35:17.410 align:middle line:84%
It will be a silly theorem,
one which I will not

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:18.970 align:middle line:90%
write the actual proof of.

00:35:18.970 --> 00:35:24.070 align:middle line:90%
But the empty set--

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:26.940 align:middle line:84%
so again, I've told
you before that

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:30.400 align:middle line:84%
when I'm writing on the
board, writing in my notes,

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:34.230 align:middle line:90%
I typically shorten words.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:38.900 align:middle line:84%
So "measurable" you will
see written as "mble"

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:42.860 align:middle line:84%
throughout the notes, and also
when I write on the board.

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:45.290 align:middle line:84%
I forget the word for what
it means to shorten words.

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:49.930 align:middle line:90%


00:35:49.930 --> 00:35:57.940 align:middle line:90%
So the empty set is measurable.

00:35:57.940 --> 00:36:01.630 align:middle line:90%
R is measurable.

00:36:01.630 --> 00:36:10.750 align:middle line:84%
And we have the fact
that a subset of R

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:19.400 align:middle line:84%
is measurable if and only if
its complement is measurable

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:24.140 align:middle line:84%
because either one of
these definitions--

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:28.880 align:middle line:84%
remember, by what we've
proven about outer measure,

00:36:28.880 --> 00:36:33.170 align:middle line:84%
this equality is equivalent
to requiring this inequality.

00:36:33.170 --> 00:36:35.700 align:middle line:90%


00:36:35.700 --> 00:36:38.780 align:middle line:84%
These are symmetric
in E and E complement.

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:41.400 align:middle line:90%


00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:44.040 align:middle line:84%
So E is measurable if and
only if its complement

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:44.880 align:middle line:90%
is measurable.

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:56.560 align:middle line:90%


00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:01.420 align:middle line:84%
All right, so these are kind
of the stupidest measurable

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:05.600 align:middle line:90%
sets you could have.

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:07.730 align:middle line:90%
Again, the empty set-- why?

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:10.070 align:middle line:84%
Because this is then empty
and the outer measure

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.350 align:middle line:90%
of the empty set is 0.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:13.880 align:middle line:84%
And then over here,
I would just get

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:16.610 align:middle line:84%
the measure of A intersect the
empty set complement, which

00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:20.810 align:middle line:84%
is R. So I get the
outer measure of A

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:22.460 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
outer measure of A.

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:24.500 align:middle line:84%
And since the empty
set is measurable,

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:26.450 align:middle line:84%
its complement R
is also measurable.

00:37:26.450 --> 00:37:29.010 align:middle line:90%


00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:39.620 align:middle line:84%
So let's do some non-stupid
examples of measurable sets,

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:43.560 align:middle line:84%
still kind of trivial because
they don't make up very much.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:58.800 align:middle line:84%
But if a set has outer measure
0, then it's measurable.

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:06.090 align:middle line:90%


00:38:06.090 --> 00:38:07.470 align:middle line:90%
So how do we prove this?

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:11.400 align:middle line:84%
Again, we just need to
prove this inequality here

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:18.990 align:middle line:84%
for every subset of R. So
let A be a subset of R. Then

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:22.440 align:middle line:84%
A intersect E, this
is contained in E.

00:38:22.440 --> 00:38:26.550 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, since we
know outer measure is--

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:29.952 align:middle line:84%
so the fancy word for
when A is a subset of B,

00:38:29.952 --> 00:38:31.410 align:middle line:84%
the outer measure
of A is less than

00:38:31.410 --> 00:38:32.910 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
outer measure of B--

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:35.610 align:middle line:84%
the fancy word for
that is "monotonicity."

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:38.280 align:middle line:84%
But since the outer
measure is monotonic,

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:41.130 align:middle line:84%
that tells me that
the outer measure of E

00:38:41.130 --> 00:38:44.760 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
A intersect E is less than

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:48.090 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
outer measure of E.

00:38:48.090 --> 00:38:52.080 align:middle line:84%
And since that is
0, that implies

00:38:52.080 --> 00:38:58.023 align:middle line:84%
that the outer measure
of A intersect E is 0.

00:38:58.023 --> 00:39:00.440 align:middle line:84%
Because the outer measure is
always a non-negative number.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:04.830 align:middle line:90%


00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:12.890 align:middle line:84%
Thus, the outer measure
of A intersect E

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:16.880 align:middle line:84%
plus the outer measure
of A intersect E

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:19.580 align:middle line:84%
complement-- this
is 0, so this is

00:39:19.580 --> 00:39:24.200 align:middle line:84%
equal to the outer measure
of A intersect E complement.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:28.130 align:middle line:84%
A intersect E complement
is contained in A.

00:39:28.130 --> 00:39:31.585 align:middle line:84%
So the outer measure of
A intersect E complement

00:39:31.585 --> 00:39:32.960 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
less than or equal

00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:35.720 align:middle line:84%
to the outer
measure of A, again,

00:39:35.720 --> 00:39:38.645 align:middle line:84%
because this set is
contained within A.

00:39:38.645 --> 00:39:42.950 align:middle line:90%
And thus, E is measurable.

00:39:42.950 --> 00:39:52.540 align:middle line:90%


00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:57.780 align:middle line:84%
So right now,
after two theorems,

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:01.320 align:middle line:84%
we've shown that the
uninteresting sets are

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:03.030 align:middle line:84%
measurable, if by
"uninteresting"

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:08.620 align:middle line:84%
we mean having very
little measure,

00:40:08.620 --> 00:40:10.540 align:middle line:84%
even though I haven't
defined measure yet.

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:11.980 align:middle line:90%
I've just defined outer measure.

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:22.010 align:middle line:84%
I'm using these terms
interchangeably, unfortunately,

00:40:22.010 --> 00:40:25.850 align:middle line:84%
but you'll see why either
by the end of this class

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:26.960 align:middle line:90%
or the end of next class.

00:40:26.960 --> 00:40:38.360 align:middle line:90%


00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:44.610 align:middle line:84%
But so far, we don't have
very many interesting examples

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:45.750 align:middle line:90%
of measurable sets.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:48.430 align:middle line:90%


00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:50.260 align:middle line:84%
What we will show
is that there's

00:40:50.260 --> 00:40:53.480 align:middle line:84%
a lot of interesting
measurable sets.

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:58.040 align:middle line:84%
In fact, every open
set is measurable,

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:02.720 align:middle line:84%
and since if an open
set is measurable

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:06.350 align:middle line:84%
its complement is measurable,
and the complements

00:41:06.350 --> 00:41:08.750 align:middle line:84%
of open sets are
closed sets, then we

00:41:08.750 --> 00:41:13.100 align:middle line:84%
have that every closed set
is measurable, as well.

00:41:13.100 --> 00:41:16.040 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, it's much richer
than that, as we'll see.

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.730 align:middle line:90%


00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:22.720 align:middle line:84%
You can take a countable
collection of open sets

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:24.040 align:middle line:90%
and take their intersection.

00:41:24.040 --> 00:41:26.590 align:middle line:84%
That's not necessarily
an open set,

00:41:26.590 --> 00:41:29.650 align:middle line:84%
so it's not clear if it's
measurable by what I just said.

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:36.030 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that this
intersection of open sets

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:37.200 align:middle line:90%
will be measurable.

00:41:37.200 --> 00:41:39.180 align:middle line:84%
And again, by
taking complements,

00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:42.240 align:middle line:84%
you get unions of closed sets,
which are not necessarily

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:46.000 align:middle line:90%
closed, are also measurable.

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:51.440 align:middle line:84%
So when I learned
measure theory,

00:41:51.440 --> 00:41:56.620 align:middle line:84%
my instructor told me that if
you can write down the set,

00:41:56.620 --> 00:41:59.800 align:middle line:90%
chances are it's measurable.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:02.440 align:middle line:84%
If you can sit
down and write down

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:06.910 align:middle line:84%
a union of intersections of
complements of so on and so on

00:42:06.910 --> 00:42:09.850 align:middle line:84%
of some basic sets,
then that's measurable.

00:42:09.850 --> 00:42:13.405 align:middle line:84%
And we'll see why
that's true shortly.

00:42:13.405 --> 00:42:17.610 align:middle line:90%


00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:22.140 align:middle line:84%
Now, before we get to showing
everything I just told you,

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:26.610 align:middle line:84%
we need some general facts
about measurable sets,

00:42:26.610 --> 00:42:31.950 align:middle line:84%
about the structure of the
collection of measurable sets.

00:42:31.950 --> 00:42:35.370 align:middle line:90%


00:42:35.370 --> 00:42:39.840 align:middle line:84%
Right now, we just know that the
collection of measurable sets

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:46.370 align:middle line:84%
includes the empty set
R and the sets that

00:42:46.370 --> 00:42:55.350 align:middle line:84%
have outer measure equal to
0, which I see I didn't write.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:57.860 align:middle line:84%
This is also the
danger of lecturing

00:42:57.860 --> 00:43:01.070 align:middle line:84%
in an empty classroom is that if
I make a mistake on the board,

00:43:01.070 --> 00:43:03.520 align:middle line:90%
there's no one to correct me.

00:43:03.520 --> 00:43:08.782 align:middle line:84%
So next, prove the following
theorem about measurable sets--

00:43:08.782 --> 00:43:10.240 align:middle line:84%
that if I have two
measurable sets,

00:43:10.240 --> 00:43:12.910 align:middle line:90%
their union is measurable.

00:43:12.910 --> 00:43:26.690 align:middle line:84%
If E1, E2 are measurable,
then their union, E1 union E2,

00:43:26.690 --> 00:43:27.590 align:middle line:90%
is measurable.

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:32.070 align:middle line:90%


00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:39.152 align:middle line:84%
So again, we have to
verify that inequality

00:43:39.152 --> 00:43:40.360 align:middle line:90%
to show that it's measurable.

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:58.870 align:middle line:84%
So let A be a subset of R.
So since E2 is measurable,

00:43:58.870 --> 00:44:08.230 align:middle line:84%
we get that the measure of A
intersect the complement of E1

00:44:08.230 --> 00:44:14.920 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer measure
of A intersect E1 complement

00:44:14.920 --> 00:44:25.000 align:middle line:84%
intersect E2 plus the outer
measure of E of A intersect E1

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.396 align:middle line:84%
complement intersect
E2 complement.

00:44:28.396 --> 00:44:31.450 align:middle line:90%


00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:34.040 align:middle line:84%
You may be asking, where
the hell did this come from?

00:44:34.040 --> 00:44:35.030 align:middle line:90%
Why do I care?

00:44:35.030 --> 00:44:38.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's because
I'm getting something.

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:47.270 align:middle line:84%
Remember, this is equal
to, by De Morgan's law,

00:44:47.270 --> 00:44:51.170 align:middle line:84%
the complement of the
union of E1 and E2.

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So I want to somehow have
some relation involving

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:57.950 align:middle line:84%
the complement of E1
union E2 intersect A.

00:44:57.950 --> 00:45:00.320 align:middle line:84%
Because again,
I'm trying to show

00:45:00.320 --> 00:45:02.720 align:middle line:84%
that the measure of
A intersect E1 union

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:07.790 align:middle line:84%
E2 plus the outer measure of A
intersect the complement of E1

00:45:07.790 --> 00:45:11.880 align:middle line:84%
union E2, which is exactly this
term, equals the measure of A.

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:16.550 align:middle line:84%
So it looks like this
relation was just grabbed out

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.420 align:middle line:90%
of left field.

00:45:17.420 --> 00:45:20.645 align:middle line:84%
But that's the thinking
behind why you would care.

00:45:20.645 --> 00:45:27.510 align:middle line:90%


00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:41.720 align:middle line:84%
So now, A, if I take A and
intersect it with E1 union E2,

00:45:41.720 --> 00:45:56.040 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to A intersect
E1 union A intersect E2.

00:45:56.040 --> 00:46:07.880 align:middle line:90%


00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:11.450 align:middle line:84%
Now, everything
from here that also

00:46:11.450 --> 00:46:15.420 align:middle line:84%
has something in common with
E1 is contained in this set.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:21.590 align:middle line:84%
So this union is actually
equal to A intersect E1 union A

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:27.800 align:middle line:84%
intersect E2 intersect
E1 complement.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:31.010 align:middle line:90%


00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:34.850 align:middle line:84%
Now, this appeared
here, so you can kind of

00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:37.700 align:middle line:84%
see maybe some magic is going
to start to happen in a minute

00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:39.680 align:middle line:84%
when we start
taking the measure.

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:44.710 align:middle line:90%
So what do we get?

00:46:44.710 --> 00:46:52.770 align:middle line:84%
We get that the measure
of A intersect E1 union

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:57.540 align:middle line:84%
E2, which is this
side, this is less than

00:46:57.540 --> 00:47:04.800 align:middle line:84%
or equal to the
outer measure of A--

00:47:04.800 --> 00:47:09.510 align:middle line:84%
so this is equal to this union,
so the outer measure of this

00:47:09.510 --> 00:47:12.120 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
sum of the outer measure of this

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:13.635 align:middle line:90%
and the outer measure of this.

00:47:13.635 --> 00:47:26.770 align:middle line:90%


00:47:26.770 --> 00:47:32.570 align:middle line:84%
Now, we use the fact
that E1 is measurable.

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:37.580 align:middle line:84%
So since E1 is measurable, the
outer measure of A intersect E1

00:47:37.580 --> 00:47:41.060 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer
measure of A minus

00:47:41.060 --> 00:47:45.230 align:middle line:84%
the outer measure of A
intersect E1 complement.

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:51.960 align:middle line:90%


00:47:51.960 --> 00:47:54.360 align:middle line:84%
Or I should say
that's backwards.

00:47:54.360 --> 00:48:07.840 align:middle line:90%


00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:10.495 align:middle line:84%
And then plus, still,
this outer measure here.

00:48:10.495 --> 00:48:27.100 align:middle line:90%


00:48:27.100 --> 00:48:32.150 align:middle line:84%
But now, we're in good shape
because what do we have?

00:48:32.150 --> 00:48:35.305 align:middle line:84%
We have this term
here appearing here.

00:48:35.305 --> 00:48:41.120 align:middle line:90%


00:48:41.120 --> 00:48:47.090 align:middle line:84%
I also have this term
here is appearing here.

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:51.460 align:middle line:84%
So subtracting this over
here and subtracting

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:56.100 align:middle line:84%
that over there, I get
that the right-hand side

00:48:56.100 --> 00:49:00.870 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the outer
measure of A minus

00:49:00.870 --> 00:49:04.980 align:middle line:84%
the outer measure
of A intersect.

00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:11.550 align:middle line:84%
And I'll just rewrite this
intersection of complements

00:49:11.550 --> 00:49:16.320 align:middle line:84%
as the complement of the
union by De Morgan's law.

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:19.950 align:middle line:84%
And remember, I started
off with the outer measure

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:23.850 align:middle line:84%
of A intersect E1
union E2, and I

00:49:23.850 --> 00:49:26.370 align:middle line:84%
showed its less than or
equal to the outer measure

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:30.570 align:middle line:84%
of A minus the outer measure
of A intersect the complement,

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:38.100 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, the outer
measure of A intersect E1, E2

00:49:38.100 --> 00:49:43.470 align:middle line:84%
plus the outer measure
of A intersect E1

00:49:43.470 --> 00:49:51.130 align:middle line:84%
union E2 complement is less than
or equal to the outer measure

00:49:51.130 --> 00:49:57.630 align:middle line:84%
of A. And that's what
we wanted to prove,

00:49:57.630 --> 00:50:02.310 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, E1
union E2 is measurable.

00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:25.220 align:middle line:90%


00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:34.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, if you can do
something for two things,

00:50:34.290 --> 00:50:35.880 align:middle line:84%
you can do something
for n things,

00:50:35.880 --> 00:50:37.530 align:middle line:84%
typically, by an
induction argument.

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:43.050 align:middle line:84%
So the previous theorem
implies the following--

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:53.410 align:middle line:84%
that if E1 up to E n
are measurable, then

00:50:53.410 --> 00:51:01.397 align:middle line:84%
this finite union k equals
1 to n E k is measurable.

00:51:01.397 --> 00:51:02.480 align:middle line:90%
And how do you prove this?

00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:03.720 align:middle line:90%
You prove it by induction.

00:51:03.720 --> 00:51:09.610 align:middle line:90%
So proof by induction--

00:51:09.610 --> 00:51:12.360 align:middle line:90%


00:51:12.360 --> 00:51:15.700 align:middle line:90%
when n equals 1--

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:17.125 align:middle line:90%
the base case-- this is clear.

00:51:17.125 --> 00:51:22.080 align:middle line:90%


00:51:22.080 --> 00:51:28.640 align:middle line:84%
Suppose-- so call
this claim star--

00:51:28.640 --> 00:51:38.790 align:middle line:84%
suppose this claim star
holds for n equals m.

00:51:38.790 --> 00:51:40.860 align:middle line:84%
Now we want to show
it holds, that this

00:51:40.860 --> 00:51:44.010 align:middle line:84%
implies that the
claim of the theorem

00:51:44.010 --> 00:51:45.585 align:middle line:90%
holds with n equals m plus 1.

00:51:45.585 --> 00:52:01.180 align:middle line:90%


00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:12.670 align:middle line:84%
So let E1 E m plus
1 be measurable.

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:16.430 align:middle line:90%


00:52:16.430 --> 00:52:22.930 align:middle line:84%
Then the union k equals
1 to m plus 1 of E k

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:27.880 align:middle line:84%
is equal to union
k equals 1 to m

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:35.350 align:middle line:90%
of E k union or E sub m plus 1.

00:52:35.350 --> 00:52:38.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, by the
induction hypothesis,

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:42.580 align:middle line:84%
a collection of m
measurable sets,

00:52:42.580 --> 00:52:44.200 align:middle line:84%
their union is
measurable, so this

00:52:44.200 --> 00:52:54.260 align:middle line:84%
is measurable by the
induction hypothesis.

00:52:54.260 --> 00:52:55.910 align:middle line:84%
And we're assuming
this is measurable.

00:52:55.910 --> 00:52:57.500 align:middle line:84%
And then by the
previous theorem,

00:52:57.500 --> 00:53:00.360 align:middle line:84%
the union of two measurable
sets is measurable.

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:04.650 align:middle line:90%


00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:06.960 align:middle line:90%
And that proves the theorem.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:15.000 align:middle line:90%


00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:23.370 align:middle line:84%
So up to this point, we've
shown two basic things

00:53:23.370 --> 00:53:25.200 align:middle line:90%
about measurable sets--

00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:26.220 align:middle line:90%
really three.

00:53:26.220 --> 00:53:29.070 align:middle line:90%
First off, it's nonempty.

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:31.770 align:middle line:84%
It's a nonempty collection
of measurable sets.

00:53:31.770 --> 00:53:35.760 align:middle line:84%
We've shown that a
set is measurable if

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:38.280 align:middle line:84%
and only if its
complement is measurable.

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:46.760 align:middle line:84%
And we've also shown that
finite unions of measurable sets

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:50.100 align:middle line:90%
are again measurable.

00:53:50.100 --> 00:54:02.400 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if I
look at the collection

00:54:02.400 --> 00:54:06.360 align:middle line:84%
of all measurable sets, this
has a very special structure

00:54:06.360 --> 00:54:09.520 align:middle line:84%
or a very general
type of structure,

00:54:09.520 --> 00:54:12.250 align:middle line:84%
which I'm now going
to elaborate on.

00:54:12.250 --> 00:54:18.270 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a pause here about
measurable sets because now,

00:54:18.270 --> 00:54:20.760 align:middle line:84%
we're going to say
a few general things

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:29.230 align:middle line:84%
about certain classes of sets or
certain classes of collections

00:54:29.230 --> 00:54:29.830 align:middle line:90%
of sets.

00:54:29.830 --> 00:54:38.415 align:middle line:84%
So let me make a
definition here.

00:54:38.415 --> 00:54:41.180 align:middle line:90%


00:54:41.180 --> 00:54:53.695 align:middle line:90%
A nonempty collection of sets--

00:54:53.695 --> 00:54:58.210 align:middle line:90%


00:54:58.210 --> 00:55:00.780 align:middle line:84%
so this is a collection
of subsets of R,

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:04.380 align:middle line:84%
so it's a subset of
the power set of R--

00:55:04.380 --> 00:55:05.355 align:middle line:90%
is an algebra.

00:55:05.355 --> 00:55:10.180 align:middle line:90%


00:55:10.180 --> 00:55:12.940 align:middle line:84%
So some of you have taken
Algebra or are taking Algebra.

00:55:12.940 --> 00:55:17.670 align:middle line:84%
So there is a notion of what
an algebra is in Algebra.

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:20.070 align:middle line:84%
Here, we say a
collection of sets

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:21.720 align:middle line:84%
is an algebra if
in some sense it's

00:55:21.720 --> 00:55:29.650 align:middle line:84%
closed under taking
complements and finite unions.

00:55:29.650 --> 00:55:32.500 align:middle line:84%
If two conditions
are satisfied--

00:55:32.500 --> 00:55:36.380 align:middle line:90%


00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:40.010 align:middle line:84%
if E is in the algebra,
then its complement

00:55:40.010 --> 00:55:54.960 align:middle line:84%
is in the algebra, and two, if
I take finitely many elements

00:55:54.960 --> 00:55:58.110 align:middle line:84%
in the algebra and
finitely many subsets

00:55:58.110 --> 00:56:05.540 align:middle line:84%
from this algebra of
subsets, then this union

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:07.280 align:middle line:90%
is also in this algebra.

00:56:07.280 --> 00:56:11.480 align:middle line:90%


00:56:11.480 --> 00:56:14.090 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is for finite unions.

00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:19.730 align:middle line:84%
You can ask, what
about infinite unions?

00:56:19.730 --> 00:56:24.480 align:middle line:84%
That's not equivalent to
finite unions, meaning that's

00:56:24.480 --> 00:56:26.310 align:middle line:90%
a stronger condition to impose.

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:30.210 align:middle line:84%
So we have a special
name for collections

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:33.600 align:middle line:90%
of subsets of that type.

00:56:33.600 --> 00:56:42.560 align:middle line:84%
We say an algebra A
is a sigma algebra--

00:56:42.560 --> 00:56:45.980 align:middle line:84%
sigma for, if you like,
sum, because we really

00:56:45.980 --> 00:56:51.450 align:middle line:90%
have a summation in mind--

00:56:51.450 --> 00:56:54.050 align:middle line:90%


00:56:54.050 --> 00:57:04.390 align:middle line:84%
if also the following, stronger
condition is satisfied--

00:57:04.390 --> 00:57:21.310 align:middle line:84%
if I have a countable
collection of subsets in A,

00:57:21.310 --> 00:57:26.950 align:middle line:84%
so a countable collection
of elements of the algebra,

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:37.710 align:middle line:84%
then their union
is in the algebra.

00:57:37.710 --> 00:57:40.820 align:middle line:84%
So an algebra is
closed under taking

00:57:40.820 --> 00:57:43.220 align:middle line:90%
complements and finite unions.

00:57:43.220 --> 00:57:47.030 align:middle line:84%
A sigma algebra is
closed under complements,

00:57:47.030 --> 00:57:50.700 align:middle line:84%
and also the stronger condition
of taking countable unions.

00:57:50.700 --> 00:57:54.200 align:middle line:84%
So of course, this condition
implies this condition--

00:57:54.200 --> 00:57:55.880 align:middle line:90%
actually, that's not yet clear.

00:57:55.880 --> 00:57:58.410 align:middle line:90%


00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:00.670 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:58:00.670 --> 00:58:03.270 align:middle line:84%
Although it's not
in the definition,

00:58:03.270 --> 00:58:06.420 align:middle line:84%
we'll see in just a
second that, in fact, this

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:10.050 align:middle line:84%
implies that the empty set has
to be a member of the algebra.

00:58:10.050 --> 00:58:13.140 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this condition
implies this condition

00:58:13.140 --> 00:58:15.030 align:middle line:84%
if you just take
finitely many of these

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:20.595 align:middle line:84%
and then take them to be
empty after some finite n.

00:58:20.595 --> 00:58:26.360 align:middle line:90%


00:58:26.360 --> 00:58:28.880 align:middle line:84%
So that's the definition
of an algebra.

00:58:28.880 --> 00:58:31.400 align:middle line:84%
That's the definition
of a sigma algebra.

00:58:31.400 --> 00:58:41.765 align:middle line:90%


00:58:41.765 --> 00:58:47.870 align:middle line:84%
Let me give a few examples
real quick or a few remarks

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:53.250 align:middle line:90%
about this.

00:58:53.250 --> 00:59:07.810 align:middle line:84%
First off, by De Morgan's laws,
which tells you the complement

00:59:07.810 --> 00:59:10.720 align:middle line:84%
of a union is an intersect
of the intersection

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:21.580 align:middle line:84%
of the complements, we get that
E1 E n in the algebra implies

00:59:21.580 --> 00:59:33.130 align:middle line:84%
that E1 intersect--
so their intersection,

00:59:33.130 --> 00:59:35.830 align:middle line:84%
so this is just for
an algebra now--

00:59:35.830 --> 00:59:45.620 align:middle line:84%
their intersection, which
is equal to the complement

00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:48.850 align:middle line:84%
of the union of
their complements.

00:59:48.850 --> 00:59:52.360 align:middle line:84%
So since each of these
is in the algebra,

00:59:52.360 --> 00:59:54.160 align:middle line:84%
the complement is
in the algebra.

00:59:54.160 --> 00:59:56.740 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the finite
union is in the algebra.

00:59:56.740 --> 00:59:59.180 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the complement
of that is in the algebra.

00:59:59.180 --> 01:00:06.410 align:middle line:84%
So not only is an algebra
closed under taking

01:00:06.410 --> 01:00:08.510 align:middle line:84%
complements and
finite unions, it's

01:00:08.510 --> 01:00:11.690 align:middle line:84%
also closed under
taking intersections,

01:00:11.690 --> 01:00:12.980 align:middle line:90%
finite intersections.

01:00:12.980 --> 01:00:17.760 align:middle line:90%


01:00:17.760 --> 01:00:30.740 align:middle line:84%
So thus, if I take some element
from my nonempty algebra,

01:00:30.740 --> 01:00:37.160 align:middle line:84%
the empty set, which is equal
to E intersect E complement,

01:00:37.160 --> 01:00:38.855 align:middle line:90%
that's in the algebra.

01:00:38.855 --> 01:00:43.240 align:middle line:90%


01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:48.100 align:middle line:84%
So let me pause for a minute and
write this a little carefully.

01:00:48.100 --> 01:00:50.620 align:middle line:84%
If I take an element
of the algebra which

01:00:50.620 --> 01:00:57.430 align:middle line:84%
is supposed to be nonempty,
then the empty set, which

01:00:57.430 --> 01:01:00.190 align:middle line:84%
is equal to E intersect
its complement,

01:01:00.190 --> 01:01:01.660 align:middle line:90%
that's in the algebra.

01:01:01.660 --> 01:01:02.770 align:middle line:90%
That's in the algebra.

01:01:02.770 --> 01:01:05.320 align:middle line:84%
The intersection of two
things in the algebra

01:01:05.320 --> 01:01:08.090 align:middle line:90%
is in the algebra.

01:01:08.090 --> 01:01:10.900 align:middle line:90%
So that's also in the algebra.

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:14.170 align:middle line:84%
And also, this
implies that R, which

01:01:14.170 --> 01:01:18.310 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the complement
of the empty set,

01:01:18.310 --> 01:01:19.550 align:middle line:90%
is also in the algebra.

01:01:19.550 --> 01:01:24.130 align:middle line:84%
So for algebras of
sets, they always

01:01:24.130 --> 01:01:26.680 align:middle line:84%
contain the empty set
for nonempty ones,

01:01:26.680 --> 01:01:29.260 align:middle line:84%
which is the only kind we're
ever going to care about.

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:31.924 align:middle line:84%
They always contain
the empty set in R.

01:01:31.924 --> 01:01:37.150 align:middle line:84%
And not only are they closed
under taking complements,

01:01:37.150 --> 01:01:40.920 align:middle line:90%
but also finite unions.

01:01:40.920 --> 01:01:46.560 align:middle line:84%
And just like we proved that for
algebras, finite intersections

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:48.930 align:middle line:84%
are also in the
algebra, you can also

01:01:48.930 --> 01:01:53.070 align:middle line:84%
prove that for a sigma algebra,
countable intersections

01:01:53.070 --> 01:01:54.580 align:middle line:90%
are also in the sigma algebra.

01:01:54.580 --> 01:01:56.340 align:middle line:90%
So let me make a point of that.

01:01:56.340 --> 01:02:02.980 align:middle line:90%


01:02:02.980 --> 01:02:17.240 align:middle line:84%
If A is a sigma algebra, then
E n, a countable collection

01:02:17.240 --> 01:02:20.540 align:middle line:84%
of elements of
the sigma algebra,

01:02:20.540 --> 01:02:28.980 align:middle line:84%
implies that this countable
intersection is also

01:02:28.980 --> 01:02:32.160 align:middle line:84%
in the algebra, again
by De Morgan's law.

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:40.840 align:middle line:90%


01:02:40.840 --> 01:02:44.440 align:middle line:84%
So why am I making all these
general definitions now?

01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:52.520 align:middle line:84%
So what we're
going to show soon,

01:02:52.520 --> 01:02:55.940 align:middle line:84%
at least in the
next lecture, we'll

01:02:55.940 --> 01:03:00.090 align:middle line:84%
soon show that if
I define script

01:03:00.090 --> 01:03:12.230 align:middle line:84%
M as a set of all
measurable subsets of R,

01:03:12.230 --> 01:03:16.490 align:middle line:84%
so this is a collection
of subsets of R,

01:03:16.490 --> 01:03:17.915 align:middle line:90%
that this is a sigma algebra--

01:03:17.915 --> 01:03:21.880 align:middle line:90%


01:03:21.880 --> 01:03:23.260 align:middle line:90%
one of the things we'll show.

01:03:23.260 --> 01:03:28.440 align:middle line:90%


01:03:28.440 --> 01:03:33.850 align:middle line:84%
Now how we're doing
this now is we

01:03:33.850 --> 01:03:37.300 align:middle line:84%
had these ideals of what
a measure should be,

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:43.700 align:middle line:84%
and we're building it up
from this way, and in the end

01:03:43.700 --> 01:03:47.540 align:middle line:84%
we're going to come up with this
collection of measurable sets,

01:03:47.540 --> 01:03:51.350 align:middle line:84%
which will have this special
structure of being a sigma

01:03:51.350 --> 01:03:52.370 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

01:03:52.370 --> 01:03:53.870 align:middle line:84%
And then our measure
will be defined

01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:57.200 align:middle line:90%
on this sigma algebra of sets.

01:03:57.200 --> 01:03:59.450 align:middle line:84%
When you go on to
general measure theory,

01:03:59.450 --> 01:04:02.660 align:middle line:90%
that is the input for you.

01:04:02.660 --> 01:04:05.840 align:middle line:84%
A measure space will
then be a collection,

01:04:05.840 --> 01:04:10.730 align:middle line:84%
a sigma algebra of subsets of
a set, with a measure on it.

01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:11.660 align:middle line:90%
That's your input.

01:04:11.660 --> 01:04:15.050 align:middle line:84%
Here, your building,
if you like,

01:04:15.050 --> 01:04:18.470 align:middle line:84%
one of the first
nontrivial measures.

01:04:18.470 --> 01:04:26.655 align:middle line:84%
That's what we're doing, one
of the most important measures,

01:04:26.655 --> 01:04:27.155 align:middle line:90%
really.

01:04:27.155 --> 01:04:30.910 align:middle line:90%


01:04:30.910 --> 01:04:33.610 align:middle line:90%
So we have that definition.

01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:37.700 align:middle line:84%
If you've taken my
class before, you

01:04:37.700 --> 01:04:41.180 align:middle line:84%
know that if there's
a definition,

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:43.370 align:middle line:84%
then we should see
an example or two.

01:04:43.370 --> 01:04:48.200 align:middle line:84%
I said that the set
of measurable sets

01:04:48.200 --> 01:04:49.310 align:middle line:90%
will be a sigma algebra.

01:04:49.310 --> 01:04:51.380 align:middle line:84%
That's going to take a
little work to get to,

01:04:51.380 --> 01:04:55.160 align:middle line:84%
but we can come up with
a few examples already.

01:04:55.160 --> 01:05:12.170 align:middle line:90%


01:05:12.170 --> 01:05:14.930 align:middle line:90%
So some simple examples--

01:05:14.930 --> 01:05:17.345 align:middle line:90%
we have the stupidest example.

01:05:17.345 --> 01:05:19.220 align:middle line:84%
Always start off with
the stupidest examples.

01:05:19.220 --> 01:05:23.280 align:middle line:90%
That's the way to go.

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:32.240 align:middle line:84%
So the simplest sigma
algebra is given

01:05:32.240 --> 01:05:35.900 align:middle line:84%
by this collection of
subsets consisting only

01:05:35.900 --> 01:05:42.850 align:middle line:84%
of the empty set in R.
The next stupidest one

01:05:42.850 --> 01:05:47.170 align:middle line:84%
is on the other end of the
spectrum, where every subset is

01:05:47.170 --> 01:05:49.660 align:middle line:90%
in this sigma algebra.

01:05:49.660 --> 01:05:56.690 align:middle line:90%
So these are sigma algebras.

01:05:56.690 --> 01:06:01.680 align:middle line:90%


01:06:01.680 --> 01:06:04.890 align:middle line:90%
What's a non-stupid one?

01:06:04.890 --> 01:06:08.790 align:middle line:84%
Let's say we take A to be
the set, the collection

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:18.590 align:middle line:84%
of all subsets E, such
that either E is countable

01:06:18.590 --> 01:06:27.570 align:middle line:90%
or E complement is countable.

01:06:27.570 --> 01:06:37.640 align:middle line:90%


01:06:37.640 --> 01:06:42.720 align:middle line:90%
So why is this?

01:06:42.720 --> 01:06:44.550 align:middle line:84%
I claim this is also
a sigma algebra.

01:06:44.550 --> 01:06:54.140 align:middle line:90%


01:06:54.140 --> 01:06:58.100 align:middle line:90%
Why is this a sigma algebra?

01:06:58.100 --> 01:07:03.650 align:middle line:84%
First off, it's clear that if E
is in A then its complement is

01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:10.210 align:middle line:84%
in A. Because if E
is countable, then

01:07:10.210 --> 01:07:13.780 align:middle line:84%
the complement of its
complement is E, is countable.

01:07:13.780 --> 01:07:17.080 align:middle line:84%
So E in A implies
that E complement

01:07:17.080 --> 01:07:20.860 align:middle line:84%
is A. In other words, this
condition is symmetric in E

01:07:20.860 --> 01:07:22.570 align:middle line:90%
and E complement.

01:07:22.570 --> 01:07:29.070 align:middle line:84%
Why is it closed under
taking countable unions?

01:07:29.070 --> 01:07:42.410 align:middle line:84%
So suppose I take a collection
of elements from my collection

01:07:42.410 --> 01:07:47.420 align:middle line:84%
here, A. I want to
show the union is in A.

01:07:47.420 --> 01:08:02.390 align:middle line:84%
Then if for all n,
E n is countable,

01:08:02.390 --> 01:08:11.850 align:middle line:84%
then the union over n of E
sub n is a countable union

01:08:11.850 --> 01:08:13.170 align:middle line:90%
of countable sets.

01:08:13.170 --> 01:08:17.430 align:middle line:84%
And therefore,
this is countable,

01:08:17.430 --> 01:08:31.220 align:middle line:84%
which implies this guy is
in the set collection A.

01:08:31.220 --> 01:08:33.439 align:middle line:84%
So this is the case,
that all of these

01:08:33.439 --> 01:08:39.430 align:middle line:84%
are countable if there
exists an integer N0 such

01:08:39.430 --> 01:08:52.899 align:middle line:84%
that E n complement is
countable, E n sub 0.

01:08:52.899 --> 01:08:59.630 align:middle line:84%
Then, remember what
I have to verify

01:08:59.630 --> 01:09:04.939 align:middle line:84%
is that the union of
E n's is in the set

01:09:04.939 --> 01:09:07.069 align:middle line:84%
A, which means
either it's countable

01:09:07.069 --> 01:09:09.660 align:middle line:90%
or its complement is countable.

01:09:09.660 --> 01:09:15.490 align:middle line:84%
So if I look at the
complement of the union,

01:09:15.490 --> 01:09:19.300 align:middle line:84%
this is then equal to,
by De Morgan's law,

01:09:19.300 --> 01:09:21.220 align:middle line:84%
the intersection
of the complements.

01:09:21.220 --> 01:09:26.330 align:middle line:84%
And this is contained
in one of these guys.

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:32.080 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this is a subset
of a countable set, which

01:09:32.080 --> 01:09:41.670 align:middle line:84%
implies that this is
countable, and which

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:55.290 align:middle line:84%
is one of the conditions of A.
And therefore, this is in A.

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:58.770 align:middle line:84%
So therefore, this
collection of subsets

01:09:58.770 --> 01:10:02.760 align:middle line:84%
of R such that E is countable
or the complement of E

01:10:02.760 --> 01:10:07.680 align:middle line:84%
is countable is a
sigma algebra of sets.

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:13.845 align:middle line:84%
It's usually referred to as
the co-countable sigma algebra.

01:10:13.845 --> 01:10:19.420 align:middle line:90%


01:10:19.420 --> 01:10:23.260 align:middle line:84%
And one can define a measure
on this collection, this sigma

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:24.550 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

01:10:24.550 --> 01:10:32.640 align:middle line:84%
But we're only interested
in Lebesgue measure, which

01:10:32.640 --> 01:10:34.170 align:middle line:84%
is going to be
defined on, as we'll

01:10:34.170 --> 01:10:39.510 align:middle line:84%
see, the sigma algebra of
measurable subsets of R,

01:10:39.510 --> 01:10:46.720 align:middle line:84%
Lebesgue measurable
subsets of R.

01:10:46.720 --> 01:10:53.725 align:middle line:90%
So let's do one more example.

01:10:53.725 --> 01:10:58.837 align:middle line:90%


01:10:58.837 --> 01:11:01.170 align:middle line:84%
This will just take a minute
and it's not too technical.

01:11:01.170 --> 01:11:02.628 align:middle line:84%
I know it's the
end of the lecture.

01:11:02.628 --> 01:11:04.980 align:middle line:84%
So maybe-- well, I mean,
you're at home watching this.

01:11:04.980 --> 01:11:08.070 align:middle line:84%
You can pause it at
some point, get a snack,

01:11:08.070 --> 01:11:09.960 align:middle line:90%
change pajamas, whatever.

01:11:09.960 --> 01:11:12.450 align:middle line:84%
But anyways, here's
one last example

01:11:12.450 --> 01:11:15.690 align:middle line:84%
of a sigma algebra, which is,
in fact, a very important sigma

01:11:15.690 --> 01:11:25.440 align:middle line:90%
algebra, which is the following.

01:11:25.440 --> 01:11:28.310 align:middle line:90%


01:11:28.310 --> 01:11:33.220 align:middle line:84%
Let capital sigma
be the collection

01:11:33.220 --> 01:11:48.880 align:middle line:84%
of all sigma algebras
containing all open sets.

01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:59.040 align:middle line:90%


01:11:59.040 --> 01:12:02.730 align:middle line:84%
So let's unwind this
definition for a minute.

01:12:02.730 --> 01:12:05.540 align:middle line:84%
So you are in this
collection of sigma algebras

01:12:05.540 --> 01:12:08.540 align:middle line:84%
if you are a sigma algebra
and you contain all

01:12:08.540 --> 01:12:15.310 align:middle line:84%
open subsets of R. So for
example, the power set of R,

01:12:15.310 --> 01:12:18.330 align:middle line:84%
which is a sigma algebra--
a trivial one-- contains

01:12:18.330 --> 01:12:20.250 align:middle line:84%
every subset of
R, so it certainly

01:12:20.250 --> 01:12:23.940 align:middle line:84%
contains every open
subset of R. This

01:12:23.940 --> 01:12:27.705 align:middle line:84%
is in this collection
of sigma algebras.

01:12:27.705 --> 01:12:37.630 align:middle line:90%


01:12:37.630 --> 01:12:46.390 align:middle line:84%
And let me define script B to be
the intersection over all sigma

01:12:46.390 --> 01:12:49.280 align:middle line:84%
algebras in this collection
of sigma algebras.

01:12:49.280 --> 01:12:53.650 align:middle line:84%
So this is the intersection of
every sigma algebra containing

01:12:53.650 --> 01:12:56.360 align:middle line:90%
all open sets.

01:12:56.360 --> 01:13:00.910 align:middle line:90%
So first off, this is nonempty.

01:13:00.910 --> 01:13:06.130 align:middle line:84%
This is a nonempty
intersection, so note, this

01:13:06.130 --> 01:13:10.540 align:middle line:84%
is a intersection of subsets
of collections of subsets of R

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:16.750 align:middle line:90%
So this is R and it's nonempty.

01:13:16.750 --> 01:13:22.270 align:middle line:90%


01:13:22.270 --> 01:13:25.380 align:middle line:90%
Then B is--

01:13:25.380 --> 01:13:27.390 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say a lot
in these next few words--

01:13:27.390 --> 01:13:41.330 align:middle line:84%
is the smallest sigma
algebra containing

01:13:41.330 --> 01:13:51.000 align:middle line:84%
all open subsets of R. And
we call it the Borel sigma

01:13:51.000 --> 01:13:51.500 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

01:13:51.500 --> 01:14:08.710 align:middle line:90%


01:14:08.710 --> 01:14:10.300 align:middle line:90%
So how do you think of this?

01:14:10.300 --> 01:14:15.820 align:middle line:84%
You take every sigma
algebra in the universe

01:14:15.820 --> 01:14:18.850 align:middle line:90%
that contain every open set.

01:14:18.850 --> 01:14:21.520 align:middle line:84%
You take the intersection
of all these sigma algebras.

01:14:21.520 --> 01:14:24.020 align:middle line:84%
My claim is that you
get a sigma algebra,

01:14:24.020 --> 01:14:26.530 align:middle line:84%
and this is the smallest
sigma algebra containing

01:14:26.530 --> 01:14:30.390 align:middle line:90%
all open subsets of R.

01:14:30.390 --> 01:14:33.090 align:middle line:84%
So another way to say
this last sentence

01:14:33.090 --> 01:14:48.570 align:middle line:84%
is, E is in sigma and for all A
in sigma, E is contained in A.

01:14:48.570 --> 01:14:51.630 align:middle line:84%
So it's the smallest
sigma algebra containing

01:14:51.630 --> 01:15:00.530 align:middle line:84%
all open subsets of R.
The proof is not hard.

01:15:00.530 --> 01:15:03.430 align:middle line:84%
You just need to make
sure you understand

01:15:03.430 --> 01:15:05.200 align:middle line:90%
exactly what I'm saying here.

01:15:05.200 --> 01:15:12.060 align:middle line:84%
And if you understand
what I'm saying here,

01:15:12.060 --> 01:15:13.580 align:middle line:90%
the proof is quite simple.

01:15:13.580 --> 01:15:16.580 align:middle line:84%
And I'm only going
to do one part.

01:15:16.580 --> 01:15:23.560 align:middle line:84%
So first off, once I show
that B is a sigma algebra,

01:15:23.560 --> 01:15:31.900 align:middle line:84%
then the rest follows because
if I take any open subset of R,

01:15:31.900 --> 01:15:34.540 align:middle line:84%
it's contained in
every one of these.

01:15:34.540 --> 01:15:36.640 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it's contained
in the intersection.

01:15:36.640 --> 01:15:39.100 align:middle line:84%
So every open
subset is contained

01:15:39.100 --> 01:15:42.010 align:middle line:84%
in B or every open subset
is an element of B.

01:15:42.010 --> 01:15:47.170 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't say contained,
but every open subset is

01:15:47.170 --> 01:15:53.210 align:middle line:84%
an element of B. And
so I just need to show

01:15:53.210 --> 01:15:54.710 align:middle line:90%
that B is a sigma algebra.

01:15:54.710 --> 01:15:58.640 align:middle line:84%
And since it's equal to the
intersection of all sigma

01:15:58.640 --> 01:16:02.340 align:middle line:84%
algebras containing
every open subset,

01:16:02.340 --> 01:16:03.870 align:middle line:90%
it has to be the smallest one.

01:16:03.870 --> 01:16:08.220 align:middle line:84%
Any other one-- this
intersection for any fixed one,

01:16:08.220 --> 01:16:11.190 align:middle line:84%
this intersection is
contained in any fixed one.

01:16:11.190 --> 01:16:15.150 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, B is contained
in any fixed sigma algebra which

01:16:15.150 --> 01:16:16.950 align:middle line:90%
contains all the open subsets.

01:16:16.950 --> 01:16:22.140 align:middle line:84%
So I just need to verify
that B is a sigma algebra.

01:16:22.140 --> 01:16:31.940 align:middle line:90%


01:16:31.940 --> 01:16:37.930 align:middle line:84%
And since I'm running out of
time, I'm going to do one part,

01:16:37.930 --> 01:16:41.590 align:middle line:84%
or verify just one
part of the definition.

01:16:41.590 --> 01:16:45.900 align:middle line:84%
Because the other part's
essentially the same except you

01:16:45.900 --> 01:16:48.840 align:middle line:90%
have to use more chalk.

01:16:48.840 --> 01:16:54.150 align:middle line:84%
So I just need to verify
that B is a sigma algebra.

01:16:54.150 --> 01:16:59.160 align:middle line:90%


01:16:59.160 --> 01:17:02.940 align:middle line:84%
Now, suppose E is
an element of B.

01:17:02.940 --> 01:17:07.140 align:middle line:84%
So this means it's
a subset of R. It's

01:17:07.140 --> 01:17:13.950 align:middle line:84%
one of these subsets of
R that's an element of B.

01:17:13.950 --> 01:17:20.910 align:middle line:84%
Then for all sigma algebras
in this collection,

01:17:20.910 --> 01:17:26.490 align:middle line:84%
since B is the intersection over
all of these sigma algebras,

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:38.300 align:middle line:84%
E is in A. And since each
of these is a sigma algebra,

01:17:38.300 --> 01:17:41.771 align:middle line:84%
I get that the
complement is in A.

01:17:41.771 --> 01:17:45.800 align:middle line:84%
And this statement
says for every sigma

01:17:45.800 --> 01:17:47.870 align:middle line:84%
algebra in this
collection, the complement

01:17:47.870 --> 01:17:52.430 align:middle line:84%
is in that sigma algebra,
and therefore, E complement

01:17:52.430 --> 01:17:59.410 align:middle line:84%
is in the intersection,
which is, remember,

01:17:59.410 --> 01:18:02.200 align:middle line:84%
how I've defined the
Borel sigma algebra.

01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:06.430 align:middle line:90%


01:18:06.430 --> 01:18:11.530 align:middle line:84%
And again, the proof of being
closed under countable unions

01:18:11.530 --> 01:18:12.760 align:middle line:90%
is sort of the same.

01:18:12.760 --> 01:18:17.290 align:middle line:84%
Take a countable collection
of elements of B. Then

01:18:17.290 --> 01:18:20.020 align:middle line:90%
this countable collection--

01:18:20.020 --> 01:18:23.770 align:middle line:84%
they all have to be elements
of A for every A in sigma.

01:18:23.770 --> 01:18:26.110 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, their union
has to be an element

01:18:26.110 --> 01:18:29.830 align:middle line:84%
of A for every A in sigma
because A is a sigma algebra.

01:18:29.830 --> 01:18:32.710 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the union
is in the intersection

01:18:32.710 --> 01:18:37.255 align:middle line:84%
over all A's which is equal
to B, the Borel sigma algebra.

01:18:37.255 --> 01:18:40.600 align:middle line:90%


01:18:40.600 --> 01:18:43.450 align:middle line:84%
So what we're working
towards and what

01:18:43.450 --> 01:18:51.100 align:middle line:84%
we're going to do next lecture
is we're going to show that M,

01:18:51.100 --> 01:18:57.190 align:middle line:84%
the collection of Lebesgue
measurable subsets of R

01:18:57.190 --> 01:19:01.750 align:middle line:84%
is a sigma algebra and it
contains the Borel sigma

01:19:01.750 --> 01:19:02.750 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

01:19:02.750 --> 01:19:09.760 align:middle line:84%
So it contains this very big
collection of subsets of R.

01:19:09.760 --> 01:19:12.820 align:middle line:84%
I mean, this is truly a very big
subset of R, like I was saying.

01:19:12.820 --> 01:19:14.740 align:middle line:90%
It contains all open subsets.

01:19:14.740 --> 01:19:16.390 align:middle line:84%
And since it's a
sigma algebra, it

01:19:16.390 --> 01:19:18.760 align:middle line:84%
contains the complements
of all open subsets, i.e.

01:19:18.760 --> 01:19:20.350 align:middle line:90%
closed subsets.

01:19:20.350 --> 01:19:24.550 align:middle line:84%
but then it also contains all
intersections of open subsets

01:19:24.550 --> 01:19:30.170 align:middle line:84%
because sigma algebras
are closed under taking

01:19:30.170 --> 01:19:32.730 align:middle line:84%
countable
intersections, as well.

01:19:32.730 --> 01:19:37.620 align:middle line:84%
And then, you could
take countable unions

01:19:37.620 --> 01:19:40.410 align:middle line:84%
of those countable
intersections of open subsets,

01:19:40.410 --> 01:19:43.300 align:middle line:90%
and then, and so on, and so on.

01:19:43.300 --> 01:19:48.030 align:middle line:84%
So you get a very rich
class of subsets of R that's

01:19:48.030 --> 01:19:50.100 align:middle line:90%
contained in the sigma algebra.

01:19:50.100 --> 01:19:54.320 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to
show is that, like I said,

01:19:54.320 --> 01:19:57.890 align:middle line:84%
the collection of Lebesgue
measurable subsets

01:19:57.890 --> 01:20:00.980 align:middle line:84%
of R, that's a sigma algebra
and it contains the Borel sigma

01:20:00.980 --> 01:20:01.500 align:middle line:90%
algebra.

01:20:01.500 --> 01:20:03.890 align:middle line:84%
So it's a very rich
class of subsets,

01:20:03.890 --> 01:20:06.380 align:middle line:84%
even though up to this
point, all we've shown

01:20:06.380 --> 01:20:10.280 align:middle line:84%
is that the empty
set R and the sets

01:20:10.280 --> 01:20:13.640 align:middle line:84%
with outer measure equal to
0 are Lebesgue measurable.

01:20:13.640 --> 01:20:15.020 align:middle line:90%
So we'll do that next time.

01:20:15.020 --> 01:20:17.650 align:middle line:90%
And I'll stop there.

01:20:17.650 --> 01:20:24.000 align:middle line:90%